 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Eviction uses 2Q: a first-use queue ordered FIFO and a reused queue ordered by recency. A completed sheet enters the tail of the first-use queue. A hit on a first-use entry promotes it to the reused queue; a hit on a reused entry makes it the most recently used.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The first-use queue is limited to a tunable share of the cache, initially 3 GiB of the 12 GiB budget; the reused queue holds the remainder. On admission, if the first-use queue would exceed its share, the oldest first-use entries are evicted until it fits. If cached JPEG data would still exceed 12 GiB, least-recently-used reused entries are evicted until it fits. A long screening therefore churns the first-use share, while sheets requested again move to the reused queue and survive the scan.
+
+Replacement bookkeeping stays private to the worker. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q: a first-use FIFO queue and a reused recency queue with a tunable size split | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q keeps sheets requested repeatedly in the reused queue while a long screening scans through many one-use sheets. LRU lets that scan evict the comparison working set. Aged frequency counters retain repeated clips but add counter maintenance and can keep a previous project's favorites after a switch. The HTTP contract, render queue concurrency, and 12 GiB budget are unchanged under all three; 2Q pays with a second queue and a size-split parameter. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. First-use share: start at 3 GiB (25% of 12 GiB); tune from pilot workload measurements.
+2. Remembered first-use keys: an entry evicted from the first-use queue and requested again is re-rendered and re-enters the first-use queue. Whether to remember recently evicted first-use keys so that such a request is admitted directly to the reused queue is undecided.
+3. Scan-only capacity: with no entries in the reused queue, the first-use queue remains capped at its share, leaving the rest of the 12 GiB unused. Confirm whether that capacity should stay idle or be available to first-use entries while the reused queue is empty.
